Setup. An (abstract) argumentation framework is a pair F = (A, R) where A is a finite set of arguments and R ⊆ A × A is an attack relation. For S ⊆ A:
- S is conflict-free if there are no a, b ∈ S with (a, b) ∈ R;
- S is admissible if it is conflict-free and for every b ∈ A with (b, a) ∈ R for some a ∈ S, there exists c ∈ S with (c, b) ∈ R;
- S is a preferred extension if it is a ⊆-maximal admissible set;
- S is a stable extension if it is conflict-free and for every b ∈ A \ S there is a ∈ S with (a, b) ∈ R;
- S is a complete extension if it is admissible and contains every argument it defends;
- S is a semi-stable extension if it is a complete extension whose range S ∪ {b : ∃ a ∈ S, (a, b) ∈ R} is ⊆-maximal among complete extensions.

For a semantics σ ∈ {st, ss, pr} (stable, semi-stable, preferred), a framework F, and a target set E ⊆ A, define the σ-characteristic char_σ(F, E) under the "update" modification type U — which allows arbitrary modifications of the attack relation R (adding or removing any attacks, on the same set of arguments A) — as the minimum number of elementary modifications needed to transform F into a framework F' such that E is contained in some σ-extension of F' (with char_σ(F, E) = ∞ if no such F' exists, and otherwise a nonnegative integer).

The spectrum is
   S(st, ss, pr, U) = { (k, l, m) ∈ (ℕ ∪ {∞})³ : ∃ (F, E) with char_st(F, E) = k, char_ss(F, E) = l, char_pr(F, E) = m }.

A triple (k, l, m) is called monotonically decreasing if k ≥ l ≥ m. Call the set of all monotonically decreasing triples the m.d.s.-complete set.

Problem. Determine S(st, ss, pr, U) exactly. In particular, decide whether S(st, ss, pr, U) is m.d.s.-complete, i.e. whether
   S(st, ss, pr, U) = { (k, l, m) : k ≥ l ≥ m }.

